592186045417 true] [17592186125011 1074 "First, we find the matrix $\\,B-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nB-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 5 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1-\\lambda  & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is $det(B-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nSince the determinant of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix is given by the product of the entries on the main diagonal, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 5 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1-\\lambda  & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(-1-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)(\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -5)(\\lambda -1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534999 17592186045417 true] [17592186192441 1074 "Two lines are parallel if they never intersect.\n\nFirst, notice that the lines $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{PQ}$ and  $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{TS}$ are parallel.\n\n![](images/78/783488aa5e48aac72fe8aa4870f9a3e5621799475286bcc820573ae099b3ed6b.png)\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the sides $\\overline{PQ}$ and $\\overline{TS}$ are parallel. We can write this as follows: \n\n$$\n\\overline{PQ} \\parallel \\overline{TS}\n$$" 13194139534999 17592186045417 true] [17592186192441 1074 "Two lines are parallel if they never intersect.\n\nFirst, notice that the lines $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{TS}$ are parallel.\n\n![](images/78/783488aa5e48aac72fe8aa4870f9a3e5621799475286bcc820573ae099b3ed6b.png)\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the sides $\\overline{PQ}$ and $\\overline{TS}$ are parallel. We can write this as follows:\n$\\overline{PQ} \\parallel \\overline{TS}$" 13194139534999 17592186045417 false] [17592186192442 1074 "![](images/35/3528edd920ddd50e0e976a46a78980708369f17f5809a3b8578af1bda683fc8b.png)\n\nTwo lines are perpendicular if they intersect and form a $90^\\circ$ angle.\n\nIn the given diagram, the only perpendicular lines are $p$ and $q$." 13194139534999 17592186045417 false] [17592186192442 1074 "![](images/35/3528edd920ddd50e0e976a46a78980708369f17f5809a3b8578af1bda683fc8b.png)\n\nTwo lines are perpendicular if they intersect and form a $90^\\circ$ angle.\n\nIn the given diagram, the only perpendicular lines are $p$ and $q.$" 13194139534999 17592186045417 true] [17592186192614 1074 "![](images/eb/ebebd8b07b11592683a280b53674fa5a153743830f833e752b5b16df1bbf809d.png)\n\nTwo lines are parallel if they never intersect.\n\nIn the given diagram, the only parallel lines are $l$ and $m$." 13194139534999 17592186045417 false] [17592186192614 1074 "![](images/eb/ebebd8b07b11592683a280b53674fa5a153743830f833e752b5b16df1bbf809d.png)\n\nTwo lines are parallel if they never intersect.\n\nIn the given diagram, the only parallel lines are $l$ and $m.$" 13194139534999 17592186045417 true] [17592186192728 1038 #edb/ref 17592186447466 13194139534999 17592186045417 true] [17592186192728 1038 #edb/ref 17592186447469 13194139534999 17592186045417 true] [17592186192728 1038 #edb/ref 17592186447471 13194139534999 17592186045417 true] [17592186192728 1038 #edb/ref 17592186447474 13194139534999 17592186045417 true] [17592186192728 1038 #edb/ref 17592186447475 13194139534999 17592186045417 true] [17592186192729 1038 #edb/ref 17592186447463 13194139534999 17592186045417 true] [17592186192729 1038 #edb/ref 17592186447464 13194139534999 17592186045417 true] [17592186192855 1038 #edb/ref 17592186447467 13194139534999 17592186045417 true] [17592186192855 1038 #edb/ref 17592186447468 13194139534999 17592186045417 true] [17592186192890 1050 420.0 13194139534999 17592186045417 true] [17592186193812 1064 "Two lines are **parallel** if they never meet. So, for example, the lines $r$ and $s$ below are parallel.\n\n![](images/d9/d9bcd3cb4e201292afd4c3dca68273f16cff43d76c5a6a2f80348f5d8db1bd4c.png)\n\nIf two lines are parallel, we can express this using the $\\parallel$ symbol as follows:\n\n$$\nr \\parallel s\n$$\n\nIn words, we say, \"the line $r$ is parallel to the line $s$.\"\n\nIf two lines are not parallel, they must have a common point. In this case, we say that the lines **intersect**.\n\nFor example, the lines $v$ and $w$ below intersect at the point $P{:}$\n\n![](images/a6/a659c4a23e60880656c4fea0ec8e6ca63e0d9e55704fdb53b5bdf1a6e30b89e3.png)\n\nWe have the following important property about lines in two-dimensional space.\n\n> *Any two distinct lines are either parallel or intersect at exactly one point.*" 13194139534999 17592186045417 false] [17592186193812 1064 "Two lines are **parallel** if they never meet. So, for example, the lines $r$ and $s$ below are parallel. \n\n![](images/d9/d9bcd3cb4e201292afd4c3dca68273f16cff43d76c5a6a2f80348f5d8db1bd4c.png)\n\nIf two lines are parallel, we can express this using the $\\parallel$ symbol as follows:\n\n$$\nr \\parallel s\n$$\n\nIn words, we say, \"the line $r$ is parallel to the line $s.$\"\n\nIf two lines are not parallel, they must have a common point. In this case, we say that the lines **intersect**.\n\nFor example, the lines $v$ and $w$ below intersect at the point $P{:}$\n\n![](images/a6/a659c4a23e60880656c4fea0ec8e6ca63e0d9e55704fdb53b5bdf1a6e30b89e3.png)\n\nWe have the following important property about lines in two-dimensional space.\n\n*Any two distinct lines are either parallel or intersect at exactly one point.*" 13194139534999 17592186045417 true] [17592186193847 1064 "Two lines are **perpendicular** if they intersect and form a $90^\\circ$ angle.\n\nFor example, the lines $r$ and $s$ below are perpendicular.\n\n![](images/73/73d2e836e942d6e468c8d9652921a9321b842d47b54ba59529123da61a9a96b4.png)\n\nWhen two lines are perpendicular, we can express this with the symbol $\\perp$, as follows:\n\n$$\nr \\perp s\n$$\n\nIn words, we say, \"the line $r$ is perpendicular to the line $s$.\"" 13194139534999 17592186045417 false] [17592186193847 1064 "Two lines are **perpendicular** if they intersect and form a $90^\\circ$ angle. \n\nFor example, the lines $r$ and $s$ below are perpendicular.\n\n![](images/73/73d2e836e942d6e468c8d9652921a9321b842d47b54ba59529123da61a9a96b4.png)\n\nWhen two lines are perpendicular, we can express this with the symbol $\\perp,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nr \\perp s\n$$\n\nIn words, we say, \"the line $r$ is perpendicular to the line $s$.\"" 13194139534999 17592186045417 true] [17592186366511 1071 "$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $D$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $D$\n- $\\lambda =-1$ is an eigenvalue of $D$\n- The eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ has algebraic multiplicity $2$" 13194139534999 17592186045417 false] [17592186366511 1071 "$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $D$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $D$\n\nII. $\\lambda =-1$ is an eigenvalue of $D$\n\nIII. The eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ has algebraic multiplicity $2$" 13194139534999 17592186045417 true] [17592186404129 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404130 13194139534999 17592186045417 false] [17592186404129 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404131 13194139534999 17592186045417 false] [17592186404129 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404132 13194139534999 17592186045417 false] [17592186404129 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404133 13194139534999 17592186045417 false] [17592186404129 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404134 13194139534999 17592186045417 false] [17592186404129 1075 #edb/ref 17592186447393 13194139534999 17592186045417 true] [17592186404129 1075 #edb/ref 17592186447401 13194139534999 17592186045417 true] [17592186404129 1075 #edb/ref 17592186447404 13194139534999 17592186045417 true] [17592186404129 1075 #edb/ref 17592186447434 13194139534999 1